      "math_academy_id": "q-103437",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011175/assets/fb61c048eb7eac87f82c53717f8f67251b59610817ddf1f267e80543c0c86959.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nI. $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-1$\n\nII. $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=-1$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=-1,$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a critical point at $x=-1$ since each stationary point is a critical point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=-1$ since $f(-1)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are true, while statement III is false.",
